PARTX(8)                     System Administration                    PARTX(8)



NNAAMMEE
       partx - tell the kernel about the presence and numbering of on-disk
       partitions

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaarrttxx [--aa|--dd|--PP|--rr|--ss|--uu] [--tt _t_y_p_e] [--nn _M:_N_] [-] _d_i_s_k

       ppaarrttxx [--aa|--dd|--PP|--rr|--ss|--uu] [--tt _t_y_p_e] _p_a_r_t_i_t_i_o_n [_d_i_s_k]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Given a device or disk-image, ppaarrttxx tries to parse the partition table
       and list its contents. It can also tell the kernel to add or remove
       partitions from its bookkeeping.

       The _d_i_s_k argument is optional when a _p_a_r_t_i_t_i_o_n argument is provided. To
       force scanning a partition as if it were a whole disk (for example to
       list nested subpartitions), use the argument "-" (hyphen-minus). For
       example:

          partx --show - /dev/sda3

       This will see sda3 as a whole-disk rather than as a partition.

       ppaarrttxx iiss nnoott aann ffddiisskk pprrooggrraamm - adding and removing partitions does not
       change the disk, it just tells the kernel about the presence and
       numbering of on-disk partitions.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----aadddd
           Add the specified partitions, or read the disk and add all
           partitions.

       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print the sizes in bytes rather than in a human-readable format.

           By default, the unit, sizes are expressed in, is byte, and unit
           prefixes are in power of 2^10 (1024). Abbreviations of symbols are
           exhibited truncated in order to reach a better readability, by
           exhibiting alone the first letter of them; examples: "1 KiB" and "1
           MiB" are respectively exhibited as "1 K" and "1 M", then omitting
           on purpose the mention "iB", which is part of these abbreviations.

       --dd, ----ddeelleettee
           Delete the specified partitions or all partitions. It is not error
           to remove non-existing partitions, so this option is possible to
           use together with large ----nnrr ranges without care about the current
           partitions set on the device.

       --gg, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line with ----sshhooww or ----rraaww.

       --ll, ----lliisstt
           List the partitions. Note that all numbers are in 512-byte sectors.
           This output format is DEPRECATED in favour of ----sshhooww. Do not use it
           in newly written scripts.

       --nn, ----nnrr _M::_N
           Specify the range of partitions. For backward compatibility also
           the format _M--_N is supported. The range may contain negative
           numbers, for example ----nnrr --11::--11 means the last partition, and ----nnrr
           --22::--11 means the last two partitions. Supported range specifications
           are:

           _M
               Specifies just one partition (e.g. ----nnrr 33).

           _M::
               Specifies the lower limit only (e.g. ----nnrr 22::).

           ::_N
               Specifies the upper limit only (e.g. ----nnrr ::44).

           _M::_N
               Specifies the lower and upper limits (e.g. ----nnrr 22::44).

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Define the output columns to use for ----sshhooww, ----ppaaiirrss and ----rraaww
           output. If no output arrangement is specified, then a default set
           is used. Use ----hheellpp to get _l_i_s_t of all supported columns. This
           option cannot be combined with the ----aadddd, ----ddeelleettee, ----uuppddaattee or
           ----lliisstt options.

       ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output all available columns.

       --PP, ----ppaaiirrss
           List the partitions using the KEY="value" format.

       --rr, ----rraaww
           List the partitions using the raw output format.

       --ss, ----sshhooww
           List the partitions. The output columns can be selected and
           rearranged with the ----oouuttppuutt option. All numbers (except SIZE) are
           in 512-byte sectors.

       --tt, ----ttyyppee _t_y_p_e
           Specify the partition table type.

       ----lliisstt--ttyyppeess
           List supported partition types and exit.

       --uu, ----uuppddaattee
           Update the specified partitions.

       --SS, ----sseeccttoorr--ssiizzee _s_i_z_e
           Overwrite default sector size.

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Verbose mode.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       LIBBLKID_DEBUG=all
           enables libblkid debug output.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
       partx --show /dev/sdb3, partx --show --nr 3 /dev/sdb, partx --show
       /dev/sdb3 /dev/sdb
           All three commands list partition 3 of _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b.

       partx --show - /dev/sdb3
           Lists all subpartitions on _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b_3 (the device is used as
           whole-disk).

       partx -o START -g --nr 5 /dev/sdb
           Prints the start sector of partition 5 on _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b without header.

       partx -o SECTORS,SIZE /dev/sda5 /dev/sda
           Lists the length in sectors and human-readable size of partition 5
           on _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_a.

       partx --add --nr 3:5 /dev/sdd
           Adds all available partitions from 3 to 5 (inclusive) on _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_d.

       partx -d --nr :-1 /dev/sdd
           Removes the last partition on _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_d.

